Each of the 9 vertices connects by a diagonal to 6 others (excluding itself and its two neighbors). Each diagonal is counted twice, so there are 9 × 6 / 2 = 27 diagonals and 27 × 26 / 2 = 351 unordered pairs.
An interior crossing uses four distinct endpoints. Conversely, each choice of four vertices forms a convex quadrilateral, and exactly one pair joining those vertices crosses inside: its two diagonals. Thus the number of crossing pairs is (9 × 8 × 7 × 6)/(4 × 3 × 2 × 1) = 126. The probability is 126/351 = 14/39.
Final answer: \dfrac{14}{39}
